      "kind": "ability",
      "name": "Steal Life",
      "actions": 2,
      "category": "offensive",
      "traits": [
        "death",
        "divine",
        "void"
      ],
      "text": "Frequency once per day\n\n---\nEffect The mask makes a quick plucking gesture as they attempt to steal a creature's life. The mask targets a living creature within 30 feet, inflicting 17d6 void damage (DC 37 basic Fortitude save save). The mask gains temporary Hit Points equal to half the void damage the target takes (after applying resistances and the like), and loses any remaining temporary Hit Points after 24 hours.",
      "frequency": {
        "max": 1,
        "per": "day"
      }
    }
  ],
  "text": "These masks focus on theft in all of its forms.\n\n---\nWhen a powerful worshipper catches Norgorber's eye, he sometimes transforms that worshipper into one of his masks—supernatural incarnations who become powerful proxies to their god but sacrifice their free will.\nOther Ancestries\nMost of the masks of Norgorber are human, but not all. A mask's statistics don't change if it's a Small creature, and they gain increased space and reach if they're Large or larger. Change ancestry traits as needed, but Speed and languages are unchanged. All masks of Norgorber possess darkvision granted them by their deity. Whether or not you grant a mask additional ancestry-based abilities is left to you to decide.\nMasks and the Afterlife\nWhen a worshipper becomes a mask, they technically die and travel to the Boneyard to be judged by Pharasma, but in this case, the process is almost instantaneous. When Norgorber chooses someone to become a mask, a swift and nearly instantaneous blurring of the creature is the only indication of their change. Like most fiends, masks are immortal unless slain through violence or misadventure. Once slain, a mask's body decays as normal, and after a variable amount of time has passed, their soul returns to the Great Beyond to accrete its quintessence into Norgorber's realm of Duskfathom."
